==Introduction==

===Abstract===

This document proposes a staged path for introducing zero-knowledge (ZK) proof support in transactions.
In the initial stage, a ZK proof commitment is embedded in a transaction output using a canonical OP_RETURN pattern, and the full proof bytes (public inputs and proof material) are published on-chain via a P2SH data-carrier reveal pattern (TX_C + TX_R), to enable immediate interoperability and chain-level observability without a consensus change.
In a later stage, this proposal is intended to migrate from OP_RETURN commitments to a dedicated reserved opcode path (for example via OP_SUCCESSx-based soft fork design), enabling native script-level validation semantics and stronger on-chain composability.

The current implementation focus is Groth16 (snarkjs/rapidsnark-compatible JSON encoding) and PLONK for interim, classical-security deployments; STARK_S2 (mode 0x02; hash/FRI-based, no pairing- or DLOG-based assumptions) is the reserved post-quantum-safe growth slot and the long-term in-process verifier target.
For post-quantum-safe spend authorization in libdogecoin '''today''', the canonical path is the already-shipping sibling Post-Quantum Signature Commitments BIP (Falcon-512, Dilithium2, and Raccoon-G-44 in-library verifiers — see "PQC-safe growth path" under Rationale).
This document is written in standard BIP format and is a sibling proposal to that Post-Quantum Signature Commitments BIP, sharing the same TX_C + TX_R staging model, the same canonical 39-byte tagged OP_RETURN shape, the same SPV scanning/log conventions, and the same explicit migration target away from classical-only primitives toward post-quantum-safe primitives.
It can be adapted to a DIP process if/when a formal DIP track is preferred.

