===Motivation===

Bitcoin-family systems increasingly need to publish ZK proofs to enable confidential balances, range proofs, set membership, and other privacy/scaling primitives. A practical deployment strategy must:

# work with existing transaction relay and wallet infrastructure;
# be deployable before introducing new consensus opcodes; and
# support incremental hardening from commitment-only publication toward full script validation semantics.

An OP_RETURN commitment-first model, paired with a P2SH data-carrier reveal for the full proof bytes, provides a low-friction deployment mechanism while ecosystem tooling (snarkjs, rapidsnark, circom, halo2, plonky2, etc.) matures.
A reserved-opcode follow-up provides a path toward first-class script integration once consensus and implementation readiness are established.

===Rationale===

The design is intentionally staged:

* '''Phase 1 (Now):''' publish a 32-byte commitment (derived from the canonical ZK payload) in canonical OP_RETURN form, and optionally publish the full proof bytes via P2SH data-carrier in TX_R.
* '''Phase 2 (Future):''' define and activate a dedicated script opcode path to validate ZK proofs in script execution, replacing commitment-only signaling.

This balances deployability and safety:

* avoids immediate consensus risk;
* enables ecosystem measurement and operational validation via tooling (including SPV scanning); and
* creates a clean upgrade target for native script verification.

This direction is closely aligned with the parallel Post-Quantum Signature Commitments BIP: the two carriers share the same TX_C + TX_R staging, the same canonical 39-byte tagged OP_RETURN shape, the same SPV scanning conventions, and the same P2SH data-carrier reveal strategy. The differences are confined to the tag and proof-system specifics.

====PQC-safe growth path====

The Groth16 and PLONK proof systems registered above (modes <code>0x00</code> and <code>0x01</code>) both rely on pairing-based assumptions on BN254 / bn128 / BN_SNARK1 / alt_bn128 (KZG for PLONK, pairing-check soundness for Groth16). Those assumptions are not post-quantum-safe: a cryptographically relevant quantum adversary can recover discrete logs in the underlying curve groups and forge proofs. Phase 1 deploys these modes as interim, classical-security tooling — useful for ecosystem readiness and SPV-level operational validation, but never as the long-term migration target.

The post-quantum-safe path for libdogecoin spend authorization is '''not''' deferred to a future build: it is already shipping in-library through the sibling Post-Quantum Signature Commitments BIP, whose Falcon-512, Dilithium2, and Raccoon-G-44 verifiers run inside libdogecoin today (see <code>contrib/mainnet_falcon_test.sh</code> and the <code>[falcon-commit] / [dilithium-commit] / [raccoong-commit]</code> mainnet logs reproduced in that BIP). Because the ZK carrier shares the PQC carrier's TX_C + TX_R staging, the same canonical 39-byte tagged OP_RETURN shape, and the same P2SH data-carrier reveal strategy, a deployer who needs '''post-quantum-safe spend authorization today''' SHOULD use the sibling PQC carrier (with its in-library Falcon/Dilithium2/Raccoon-G-44 signature commitments) and treat the ZK carrier's Groth16 / PLONK modes as orthogonal evidence (e.g. range proofs, set-membership proofs) rather than as the authoritative authorization primitive.

For the ZK carrier itself, the in-tree post-quantum-safe growth slot is mode <code>0x02</code> <code>DOGECOIN_ZK_MODE_STARK_S2</code> — a hash/FRI-based proof system whose soundness rests on collision resistance and the random oracle model alone, with no pairing or DLOG assumption that a CRQC can break. The verifier-support matrix in §"Prover / Verifier Support Matrix" reserves this slot today and defers a native in-process implementation; once an in-tree STARK_S2 verifier lands, it will be co-sequenced with the PQC carrier's Phase-2 reserved-opcode track so that the ZK and PQC sides of the migration share a single OP_SUCCESSx allocation block and a single PQ activation window. The Phase-2 reserved-opcode design (see "Phase 2: Reserved Opcode Upgrade Path" below) explicitly targets that PQC-safe verifier path; classical Groth16 / PLONK script-level validation is '''not''' a Phase-2 goal of this BIP.

==Specification==

===Phase 1: OP_RETURN Commitment Encoding===

For Phase 1, ZK commitments are encoded in outputs using a canonical tagged script form:

<source>
ZK carrier:  0x6a 0x25 0x445a4b43 <mode-byte> <32-byte-commitment>   ; "DZKC"
</source>

Where:

* <code>0x6a</code> is OP_RETURN
* <code>0x25</code> is push-37
* <code>0x445a4b43</code> is the ZK carrier identifier (<code>"DZKC"</code> in ASCII)
* <code>mode-byte</code> identifies the proof system (see "Mode Identifiers" below)
* payload is exactly 4-byte tag + 1-byte mode + 32-byte commitment (37 bytes pushed; 39 bytes scriptPubKey total)

Non-canonical encodings are invalid for commitment interpretation under this proposal. In particular, mode-byte values outside the registered allow-list MUST be rejected by SPV scanners (see "Mode Identifiers").

For implementation namespace clarity, libdogecoin defines identifier constants:

<source>
DOGECOIN_ZK_OPRETURN_TAG       = "DZKC"
DOGECOIN_ZK_OPRETURN_TAG_LEN   = 4
DOGECOIN_ZK_OPRETURN_DATA_LEN  = 4 (tag) + 1 (mode) + 32 (commit) = 37
</source>

====Mode Identifiers====

The mode byte selects the proof system used by the off-chain verifier. libdogecoin currently defines the following allow-listed values; SPV scanners MUST reject other values as malformed:

{|
! Mode byte !! Constant !! Proof system !! Encoding
|-
| 0x00 || <code>DOGECOIN_ZK_MODE_GROTH16</code> || Groth16 over BN254 (snarkjs/rapidsnark) || JSON-encoded public inputs and proof
|-
| 0x01 || <code>DOGECOIN_ZK_MODE_PLONK</code>   || PLONK over BN254 (snarkjs) || JSON-encoded public inputs and proof
|-
| 0x02 || <code>DOGECOIN_ZK_MODE_STARK_S2</code>|| STARK (S-two; hash/FRI, post-quantum-safe) || implementation-defined
|}

The pairing-friendly curve used by both <code>DOGECOIN_ZK_MODE_GROTH16</code> and <code>DOGECOIN_ZK_MODE_PLONK</code> is the BN254 / Barreto–Naehrig curve at the 254-bit security parameter. The same curve appears in libdogecoin and the broader ecosystem under four conventional labels which '''MUST''' be treated as synonymous when interpreting on-chain reveals:

* '''BN254''' — the academic name and the label used throughout this BIP's prose and in the spvnode SPV decode log (e.g. <code>mode=0(groth16-bn254)</code>).
* '''bn128''' — the canonical snarkjs/circom JSON label. This is the literal string that appears in every embedded <code>verification_key.json</code> and <code>proof.json</code> in a ZKP1 reveal: <code>"curve": "bn128"</code>. The on-chain wire format is authoritatively the snarkjs label.
* '''BN_SNARK1''' — the herumi/mcl curve identifier (<code>--with-mcl[=DIR]</code> backend).
* '''alt_bn128''' — the Ethereum precompile name; cited for cross-ecosystem clarity.

Verifiers MUST reject reveals whose embedded <code>"curve"</code> field is anything other than <code>"bn128"</code> for modes 0x00 and 0x01.

Future modes MAY be added by amending this BIP. Mode-byte assignments are reserved by this proposal; deployers SHOULD NOT use unassigned values for production proofs.

====Commitment Derivation====

The commitment is the SHA256 double-hash of the canonical ZK payload bytes:

<source>
commitment32 = SHA256d(ZK_PAYLOAD)
             = SHA256(SHA256(ZK_PAYLOAD))
</source>

Where <code>ZK_PAYLOAD</code> is the canonical encoding defined in "Phase 1: ZK Payload Encoding (ZKP1)" below. This binds the commitment to the exact byte sequence revealed in TX_R, so any tampering with public inputs, proof bytes, or — for v1 payloads — the embedded verification key invalidates the commitment match.

Phase 1 defines two on-chain wire formats for the verification key (<code>vk</code>):

* '''ZKP1 v0 (legacy):''' the version byte at offset 5 is <code>0x00</code> and the canonical payload contains only <code>(magic, mode, version, circuit_id, public_inputs, proof)</code>. The verifier must obtain <code>vk</code> through an out-of-band channel (signed releases, a separate registry transaction, etc.). v0 is retained for backwards compatibility with reveals published before this spec stabilized; new deployments MUST NOT emit v0.
* '''ZKP1 v1 (self-contained reveal, RECOMMENDED for new deployments):''' the version byte at offset 5 is <code>0x01</code> and the canonical payload appends a trailing <code>VK_LEN4 || VK</code> block after the proof. v1 reveals are fully self-contained: a verifier with no access to the original <code>verification_key.json</code> can reassemble the ZKP1 payload from TX_R, extract the embedded vk, and re-run <code>snarkjs &lt;system&gt; verify</code> end-to-end against on-chain bytes alone. Because the vk is hashed into <code>SHA256d(ZK_PAYLOAD) == commit32</code>, swapping the vk after publication invalidates the commitment match — the consensus-relevant binding is preserved.

Implementations MUST accept both versions and SHOULD emit v1 by default for new deployments. v0 is retained for backwards compatibility with existing on-chain pairs.

===Phase 1: ZK Payload Encoding (ZKP1)===

The canonical ZK payload format ("ZKP1") is the byte sequence that appears in TX_R carrier scriptSigs and is hashed to produce the OP_RETURN commitment. Layout:

<source>
ZK_PAYLOAD := MAGIC4 || MODE1 || VERSION1 || CIRCUIT_ID4 || PIL2 || PUBLIC_INPUTS
              || PROOF_LEN4 || PROOF
              [ || VK_LEN4 || VK ]            ; present iff VERSION1 == 0x01

MAGIC4        : ASCII "ZKP1"            (4 bytes, DOGECOIN_ZK_CARRIER_MAGIC)
MODE1         : mode byte               (1 byte, matches OP_RETURN mode-byte)
VERSION1      : payload version         (1 byte; 0x00 = v0 legacy, 0x01 = v1 vk-included)
CIRCUIT_ID4   : application circuit ID  (4 bytes, big-endian uint32)
PIL2          : public_inputs length    (2 bytes, big-endian uint16; max 65535)
PUBLIC_INPUTS : opaque bytes            (PIL2 bytes; for Groth16/PLONK: snarkjs public.json)
PROOF_LEN4    : proof length            (4 bytes, big-endian uint32; max 2^25 = 32 MiB)
PROOF         : opaque bytes            (PROOF_LEN4 bytes; for Groth16/PLONK: snarkjs proof.json)
VK_LEN4       : (v1 only) vk length     (4 bytes, big-endian uint32; max 2^25 = 32 MiB)
VK            : (v1 only) vk bytes      (VK_LEN4 bytes; for Groth16/PLONK: snarkjs verification_key.json)
</source>

Header constants:

<source>
DOGECOIN_ZK_CARRIER_MAGIC      = "ZKP1"
DOGECOIN_ZK_CARRIER_MAGIC_LEN  = 4
DOGECOIN_ZK_CARRIER_HDR_FIXED  = 4 (magic) + 1 (mode) + 1 (version) + 4 (circuit_id) + 2 (pi_len) = 12
DOGECOIN_ZK_PAYLOAD_VERSION_V0 = 0x00 (legacy, vk distributed out-of-band)
DOGECOIN_ZK_PAYLOAD_VERSION_V1 = 0x01 (self-contained reveal — vk embedded in payload)
</source>

Encoder/decoder rules:

* The <code>MODE1</code> byte in <code>ZK_PAYLOAD</code> MUST equal the <code>mode-byte</code> in the corresponding OP_RETURN commitment.
* The <code>VERSION1</code> byte at offset 5 selects the wire format: <code>0x00</code> denotes v0 (no embedded vk); <code>0x01</code> denotes v1 (self-contained reveal with trailing <code>VK_LEN4 || VK</code>).
* Implementations MUST reject payloads whose <code>VERSION1</code> byte is not in <code>{0x00, 0x01}</code>.
* Implementations MUST reject payloads with trailing bytes after the last declared field, so v0 decoders cannot silently ignore an appended v1 vk and produce a different commit32 than v1 decoders.
* <code>CIRCUIT_ID4</code> is application-assigned and need not be globally unique; it is intended to disambiguate multiple proof contexts under the same proof system at the application layer.
* All multi-byte integer fields are big-endian.
* Implementations MUST reject payloads where the magic does not match, the mode is not allow-listed, the public-inputs section is truncated, the proof section is truncated, or (for v1) the vk section is truncated.
* Implementations MUST cap <code>PROOF_LEN4</code> and <code>VK_LEN4</code> at 2<sup>25</sup> bytes (32 MiB) each to keep size fields sane and chunking bounded; <code>PIL2</code> is naturally capped at 65535 bytes by its width.
* New deployments MUST emit v1 so a TX_R reveal alone is sufficient to verify the proof; v0 is retained for legacy interop only.

The contrib helper <code>contrib/zk_carrier/witness_helper.py</code> produces ZKP1 payloads byte-identical to the libdogecoin C codec (<code>dogecoin_zk_encode_payload</code>); supplying <code>--vkey</code> emits v1, and <code>--no-embed-vk</code> opts back into v0. This round-trip is enforced by the unit tests in <code>test/zk_carrier_tests.c</code> (<code>test_zk_codec_roundtrip</code> for v0 and <code>test_zk_codec_roundtrip_with_vk</code> for v1, including a tamper-detection check that flipping any byte of the embedded vk changes <code>commit32</code>).

===Phase 1: On-chain ZK Verification Material via P2SH Data Carrier Outputs===

Dogecoin does not support SegWit witness relay, and extra pushes in a P2PKH spending input scriptSig are non-standard under current relay policy.

The canonical Phase 1 mechanism is the small tagged OP_RETURN commitment.

A standard P2SH data-carrier pattern (TX_C + TX_R) MAY be used to publish the full <code>ZK_PAYLOAD</code> on-chain, but is OPTIONAL and subject to standard relay limits.

Implementations MUST NOT assume that the full ZK payload is present on-chain; in commitment-only mode, the verifier obtains <code>ZK_PAYLOAD</code> through an out-of-band channel (typically the wallet that produced the proof).

The P2SH carrier reveal SHOULD only be used when the encoded payload fits within standard transaction policy limits (per-push <=520 bytes; carrier scriptSig size dominated by per-input chunk count). Very large STARK proofs may require commitment-only publication.

====Phase 1: Commitment Output====

The commitment output remains canonical tagged OP_RETURN, as defined above:

<source>
ZK carrier:  0x6a 0x25 0x445a4b43 <mode-byte> <32-byte-commitment>   ; "DZKC"
</source>

Commitment derivation:

<source>
commitment32 = SHA256d(ZK_PAYLOAD)
</source>

====Phase 1: P2SH Carrier Outputs in TX_C====

TX_C creates one or more P2SH outputs that will later be spent to reveal the full ZK material:

<source>
scriptPubKey = OP_HASH160 <HASH160(redeemScript_carrier)> OP_EQUAL
</source>

The carrier <code>redeemScript</code> is intentionally small and standard. It exists only to clean the stack and return true; it carries no secrets and is reused across all ZK and PQC carrier variants:

<source>
redeemScript_carrier:
  OP_DROP repeated 5 times (TAG8, HDR8, CHUNK0, CHUNK1, CHUNK2), then OP_TRUE.
</source>

Constraints that matter for standardness include:

* The pushed redeemScript is limited in size (520 bytes) and must execute to a single true stack element (clean stack).
* Individual pushed stack elements are limited to 520 bytes, so the ZK payload must be chunked into <=520-byte pieces.

libdogecoin canonical carrier uses up to three data pushes per input (giving about 1560 bytes of usable payload per carrier spend input plus small headers); larger payloads use multiple carrier inputs in TX_R, indexed by <code>part_index</code> in HDR8.

====Phase 1: Reveal scriptSig format in TX_R====

TX_R spends each carrier output and reveals ZK material in push-only scriptSig, followed by the redeemScript. The scriptSig layout is identical to the PQC carrier reveal, with the tag changed to identify ZK content:

<source>
scriptSig pushes (push-only):
  <TAG8> <HDR8> <CHUNK0> <CHUNK1> <CHUNK2> <redeemScript_carrier>

TAG8:
  "ZKP1FULL"  (8 bytes, ASCII)

HDR8 (8 bytes):
  [0]   version    = 0x01
  [1]   part_index (0..part_total-1)
  [2]   part_total
  [3]   reserved   = 0x00
  [4..5] payload_len_lo (big-endian uint16, low 16 bits of total ZK_PAYLOAD length)
  [6..7] payload_len_hi (big-endian uint16, high 16 bits — supports payloads up to 2^32-1 bytes)

Payload:
  ZK_PAYLOAD as defined above ("ZKP1" || mode || ... || proof) is split into parts.
  Each part is carried in up to three <=520-byte CHUNK pushes.
  Empty chunks are encoded as OP_0 pushes.

redeemScript_carrier:
  OP_DROP repeated 5 times, then OP_TRUE.
</source>

The reveal scriptSig uses a single 8-byte tag (<code>ZKP1FULL</code>) regardless of mode; the mode and circuit_id are encoded inside the reassembled <code>ZK_PAYLOAD</code> via its own header. This keeps the on-chain data carrier mode-agnostic so that adding a new proof system does not require a new tag.

====Phase 1: Verification using on-chain data only====

Given TX_R:

# Parse each carrier spend input scriptSig, extract (TAG8, HDR8, CHUNKS), and reassemble <code>ZK_PAYLOAD</code> by ordering parts by <code>part_index</code> and concatenating their chunks.
# Truncate the reassembled buffer to the total payload length encoded in HDR8.
# Recompute <code>commitment32 = SHA256d(ZK_PAYLOAD)</code> and match it against TX_C canonical tagged OP_RETURN.
# Decode <code>ZK_PAYLOAD</code> per the ZKP1 layout, dispatch to the proof system selected by <code>MODE1</code>, and verify the proof against the encoded public inputs and a verification key. For v1 payloads (<code>VERSION1 == 0x01</code>) the verification key MUST be taken from the embedded <code>VK</code> field of the same payload, so verification uses only on-chain bytes. For v0 payloads (<code>VERSION1 == 0x00</code>) the verifier obtains <code>vk</code> through an out-of-band channel (signed releases, a separate registry transaction, etc.).

This yields a fully on-chain verifiable Phase 1 proof publication record, without SegWit and without non-standard extra pushes in a P2PKH spending input.

===Phase 1: Base Transaction Binding===

The ZK reveal MUST be cryptographically bound to the funding (commitment-bearing) transaction so that an attacker cannot lift a published <code>(proof, vk, public_inputs)</code> tuple from one TX_C+TX_R pair and replay it under a different funding tx. This mirrors the binding that the PQC carrier obtains "for free" from its signature: in PQC, the Falcon/Dilithium signature inside the reveal is computed over <code>tx_base</code>'s sighash and so is intrinsically tied to a specific funding tx.

For ZK reveals, libdogecoin enforces the same model by including the funding-tx sighash as a public input to the proof:

* '''<code>tx_binding</code> public input (REQUIRED for new deployments).''' The prover MUST include a 256-bit field-element <code>tx_binding</code> as the third element of the <code>PUBLIC_INPUTS</code> array (after the two existing range-proof bounds <code>low</code>, <code>high</code> in the canonical range-proof circuit). The value is computed as:
::<code>tx_binding = sighash32(tx_base, signer_p2pkh_spk, in_index = 0, sighashtype = SIGHASH_ALL)</code>
::with the most significant byte zeroed (<code>tx_binding[0] = 0x00</code>) so the value is an unambiguous 248-bit BN254 scalar. This trims at most 8 bits from a 256-bit collision-resistant digest, leaving 2^248 work for any preimage attack — comfortably beyond what is relevant for a tx-binding tag.
* '''<code>tx_base</code> definition.''' <code>tx_base</code> is TX_C with the OP_RETURN commitment output and all P2SH carrier outputs stripped, and the carrier-fee koinu re-added to the first remaining (signer-owned) output. This matches exactly how the PQC carrier defines <code>tx_base</code> for its signature, so the same <code>such -c tx_sighash32 -x &lt;tx_base&gt; -s &lt;signer_spk&gt; -i 0 -h 1</code> command-line invocation produces the binding for both schemes.
* '''Verifier obligation.''' On reveal, an SPV node (or any verifier walking the chain) MUST recompute <code>tx_binding</code> from the matched TX_C bytes it has buffered and compare against <code>PUBLIC_INPUTS[2]</code> of the recovered ZK payload. A mismatch MUST be treated as an invalid reveal — the proof was lifted from another funding tx — and surfaced via the <code>[zk-commit] tx_binding mismatch</code> log line below.
* '''Compatibility window.''' A v0/v1 payload whose <code>PUBLIC_INPUTS</code> JSON contains fewer than three array elements is interpreted as predating tx-base binding; SPV emits <code>[zk-commit] tx_binding skipped: expected >=3 public inputs</code> and falls back to OP_RETURN-only single-shot replay protection. New deployments MUST emit three public inputs.

This binding is enforced jointly at three layers, each of which independently rejects a replayed proof:

# the prover ''commits'' to <code>tx_binding</code> as a circuit public input — snarkjs <code>verify</code> rejects the proof under any other value;
# the SPV node ''recomputes'' <code>tx_binding</code> from the actual TX_C bytes it observed on chain and compares against the value carried in the reveal's public inputs (logged as <code>[zk-commit] tx_binding match|mismatch</code>);
# the publication layer's OP_RETURN-then-reveal pattern still anchors a given <code>commitment32</code> to a unique TX_C and its carrier UTXOs, providing belt-and-suspenders single-shot semantics at the chain level.

The OP_RETURN-then-reveal pattern alone (without the <code>tx_binding</code> public input) is INSUFFICIENT for cross-tx replay protection, because a second TX_C carrying the same proof bytes can still be published before the original is revealed; the <code>tx_binding</code> public input closes that window by making the proof itself tx-specific.

Current libdogecoin behavior implements all three layers:

* <code>contrib/zk_carrier/witness_helper.py --tx-binding-hex &lt;hex&gt;</code> threads the binding into the snarkjs witness as the circuit's <code>tx_binding</code> input;
* <code>such -c tx_sighash32 -x &lt;tx_base&gt; -s &lt;signer_spk&gt; -i 0 -h 1</code> exposes the canonical sighash computation to scripts and external auditors;
* <code>dogecoin_zk_compute_tx_base_sighash(tx_c, signer_spk, carrier_spk, out)</code> (in <code>src/zk_carrier/zk_commit.c</code>) is the single source of truth for "given a TX_C, what tx_binding do we expect?", and is invoked both by the SPV scanner and by the unit test <code>test_zk_tx_base_sighash_binding</code>;
* <code>such -c zk_add_commit_and_carrier_tx</code> appends the OP_RETURN commitment and all P2SH carrier outputs in a single operation;
* <code>dogecoin_zk_extract_carrier_payload</code> reassembles <code>ZK_PAYLOAD</code> from TX_R scriptSigs;
* <code>dogecoin_zk_get_commitment_hash</code> recomputes <code>SHA256d(ZK_PAYLOAD)</code> for cross-checking against the TX_C OP_RETURN commitment;
* <code>dogecoin_zk_verify_proof</code> dispatches to the proof-system-specific verifier when <code>HAVE_RAPIDSNARK</code> is defined, and returns <code>DOGECOIN_ZK_ERR_DELEGATED</code> / <code>DOGECOIN_ZK_ERR_NOT_IMPLEMENTED</code> otherwise.

Implementations MAY support additional ZK proof systems in future revisions, but MUST preserve canonical on-chain encoding rules (DZKC tag, allow-listed mode bytes, ZKP1 magic, big-endian length fields) and the <code>tx_binding</code> public-input convention defined here.

===Phase 1: SPV Validation Behavior===

SPV implementations should:

* parse transactions in scanned blocks;
* detect canonical tagged ZK commitment scripts (<code>0x6a 0x25 "DZKC" <mode> <32B></code>) with mode bytes drawn from the allow-list;
* treat only canonical encodings as valid commitments;
* surface deterministic commitment logs/telemetry for downstream verification workflows; and
* when carrier-mode P2SH data is present, perform on-chain commitment validation by extracting the ZK payload from TX_R scriptSigs and recomputing <code>SHA256d(ZK_PAYLOAD)</code> to match against buffered TX_C OP_RETURN commitments.

====SPV Commitment Validation Sources====

SPV commitment detection operates in two modes depending on how the ZK payload is published:

# '''Commitment-only:''' The commitment is detected in the OP_RETURN output. No ZK verification material is available on-chain. Log entry includes <code>source=op_return</code>, and the entry remains "Pending" until matched.
# '''Cross-TX P2SH carrier (TX_C + TX_R):''' The commitment OP_RETURN is in TX_C, and the full <code>ZK_PAYLOAD</code> is revealed in TX_R's carrier scriptSig spending the P2SH outputs. The SPV scanner reassembles the payload, recomputes <code>SHA256d(ZK_PAYLOAD)</code>, and matches against buffered OP_RETURN commitments. Log entry includes <code>source=carrier_scriptsig matched_txc_txpos=... payload_len=... txr_txid=...</code>.

In all validated cases, the log emits the algorithm label (<code>[zk-commit]</code>), block height, transaction position, the 32-byte commitment hex, the mode byte, and the validation source.

====SPV Carrier Commitment Validation Algorithm====

Given a TX_R with carrier-tagged scriptSig(s):

# For each vin, attempt to parse its scriptSig and extract the 8-byte <code>ZKP1FULL</code> tag, part metadata (part_index, part_total, payload_len), and raw payload chunks.
# If <code>part_total > 1</code>, collect chunks from all matching <code>ZKP1FULL</code>-tagged vins indexed by <code>part_index</code> and concatenate in order. Truncate to <code>payload_len</code> bytes.
# Validate the ZKP1 header (magic = "ZKP1", allow-listed mode byte, length fields self-consistent).
# Recompute <code>commitment32 = SHA256d(ZK_PAYLOAD)</code> via <code>dogecoin_zk_get_commitment_hash</code>.
# Cross-validate: match the recomputed commitment against buffered OP_RETURN commitments from TX_C transactions (current block plus a small look-back window). If matched, report <code>matched_txc_txpos</code> identifying the originating commitment transaction.
# Optionally invoke <code>dogecoin_zk_verify_proof(payload, payload_len, NULL, 0)</code> to verify the proof in-process when libdogecoin was built with <code>--with-rapidsnark</code>; otherwise log "DELEGATED" and rely on the commit-match alone (analogous to PQC's commit-match-before-signature-verify intermediate state).
# Log the validation result including <code>payload_len</code>, mode, and TX_R txid for diagnostic and audit purposes.

====SPV Log Markers====

The SPV scanner emits the following deterministic log lines for downstream tooling to grep against:

<source>
[zk-commit] Pending at height=H txpos=P commit=C mode=M source=op_return
[zk-commit] Valid at height=H txpos=P commit=C mode=M source=carrier_scriptsig matched_txc_txpos=Pc payload_len=L txr_txid=T
[zk-commit] ZK verification PASSED at height=H txpos=P mode=M
[zk-commit] ZK verification DELEGATED at height=H txpos=P mode=M err=E
[zk-commit] ZK verification FAILED at height=H txpos=P mode=M err=E
[zk-commit] Reveal validated: TX_R=T commit=C payload_len=L mode=M height=H
[zk-commit] tx_binding match txr_txid=T recomputed=R public_input[2]=P
[zk-commit] tx_binding mismatch txr_txid=T recomputed=R public_input[2]=P
[zk-commit] tx_binding skipped: expected >=3 public inputs (got N) — payload predates tx-base binding
</source>

The format intentionally mirrors the PQC <code>[falcon-commit]</code> / <code>[dilithium-commit]</code> / <code>[raccoong-commit]</code> markers so that a single log-grep harness can drive both demos.

====Verification Levels====

Three verification levels are emitted, mirroring the PQC carrier's commit-match-before-signature-verify staging:

# '''PASSED:''' libdogecoin was built with <code>--with-rapidsnark</code> or <code>--with-mcl</code> (or another in-process verifier) and the proof was checked locally against the encoded public inputs.
# '''DELEGATED:''' the verifier is not compiled in and verification is left to off-box tooling. The commitment-match (Step 5 above) still holds, providing equivalent assurance to PQC's "commit_ok before sig_verify" intermediate state — the on-chain payload provably hashes to the published commitment.
# '''FAILED:''' the verifier ran and rejected the proof.

In all three cases, the <code>Reveal validated</code> line is emitted on PASSED or DELEGATED, on the rationale that the cryptographic commitment binding is the consensus-relevant assertion at Phase 1; in-process proof verification is a defense-in-depth check for nodes that opt in.

====Carrier Sizes (illustrative)====

ZK proof sizes vary widely by proof system; the following are typical ranges for common Groth16 / PLONK / STARK contexts:

{|
! Proof system !! Proof size !! Public inputs !! Total payload !! Carrier parts !! Carrier Tag
|-
| Groth16 (BN254 / snarkjs <code>"curve": "bn128"</code>) || ~1 KB JSON || application-defined (small) || ~1.2 KB JSON || 1 || <code>ZKP1FULL</code>
|-
| PLONK (snarkjs) || ~3–5 KB JSON || application-defined || ~5 KB JSON || 4–5 || <code>ZKP1FULL</code>
|-
| STARK_S2 || tens to hundreds of KB || application-defined || tens to hundreds of KB || dozens || <code>ZKP1FULL</code>
|}

Each carrier vin scriptSig carries up to 3 chunks of ≤520 bytes (~1560 bytes of payload per part, matching the PQC carrier).

====Transaction Fee Guidance====

For carrier mode transactions, fees follow Dogecoin's per-kB minimum relay rate (100000 koinu/kB ≡ 0.001 DOGE/kB) and should include sufficient margin for prompt miner inclusion:

* '''TX_C''' (commitment + carrier outputs): minimum relay fee is 100000 koinu (0.001 DOGE), matching Dogecoin's default <code>minrelaytxfee</code>. With dust-sized inputs (~1.2 KB UTXO + carrier outputs) recommend 2000000 koinu (0.02 DOGE).
* '''TX_R''' (reveal, spending carrier outputs): minimum relay fee scales with transaction size at 100000 koinu/kB. Examples: Groth16 TX_R (~2 KB, 1 carrier vin) → 2000000 koinu (0.02 DOGE); PLONK TX_R (~6 KB, 4–5 carrier vins) → 10000000 koinu (0.1 DOGE); large STARK TX_R (tens of KB) → fee scaled accordingly per kB.
* '''Carrier output value:''' each P2SH carrier output must be at least 100000000 koinu (1 DOGE) to clear the dust-output threshold and provide enough funding for the TX_R fee when spent.

Properly-sized fees produce TX_C and TX_R confirmations within minutes on mainnet under typical relay conditions.

===Phase 2: Reserved Opcode Upgrade Path===

This proposal reserves a follow-up soft-fork design track to migrate from OP_RETURN signaling to native script validation semantics using a reserved opcode approach.
The Phase 2 document is expected to define:

* exact opcode assignment path (and its relationship to Phase 2 of the PQC carrier proposal, which may share an OP_SUCCESSx range);
* script format for ZK verification data (vk handling, public-input encoding, mode tagging);
* script evaluation and failure semantics; and
* activation/deployment process.

===Verification Transition Model (off-chain → in-script)===

This proposal is intentionally transitional:

* '''Current implementation state (Phase 1):''' transactions remain spend-authorized by existing secp256k1 script rules; ZK proofs are published/committed as parallel evidence and verified out-of-band (or in-process when libdogecoin is linked against rapidsnark).
* '''Target state (Phase 2):''' script execution validates ZK proofs directly (via reserved-opcode soft-fork path), allowing application protocols to gate spends on proof validity at consensus level.

In Phase 1, ZK commitments should be treated as an interoperability and migration bridge:

# Build the witness off-chain (e.g. via <code>contrib/zk_carrier/witness_helper.py</code>).
# Run a snarkjs/rapidsnark prover to obtain <code>proof.json</code> and <code>public.json</code>.
# Encode the canonical <code>ZK_PAYLOAD</code> ("ZKP1" || mode || circuit_id || public_inputs || proof).
# Publish <code>SHA256d(ZK_PAYLOAD)</code> in canonical tagged OP_RETURN via TX_C, and (optionally) publish the full <code>ZK_PAYLOAD</code> on-chain via the P2SH carrier in TX_R.
# SPV nodes detect the commitment, reassemble the payload from TX_R, recompute the commitment, and (if rapidsnark is linked in) verify the proof in-process.

This mirrors current implementation behavior:

* <code>such</code> exposes <code>zk_commit</code>, <code>zk_add_commit_and_carrier_tx</code>, and <code>zk_extract_carrier</code> commands;
* libdogecoin links rapidsnark when configured with <code>--with-rapidsnark</code>, enabling in-process Groth16 verification via <code>dogecoin_zk_verify_proof</code>; otherwise verification is delegated to off-box tooling;
* SPV commitment detection validates canonical encoding and emits deterministic <code>[zk-commit]</code> log lines.

Until Phase 2 activation, secp256k1 consensus checks remain authoritative for spend validity; ZK proof checks are verifier-side and policy/audit driven.

==Backwards Compatibility==

Phase 1 is backward-compatible because OP_RETURN outputs and P2SH outputs are already standard, and spendability semantics are unchanged.
Legacy nodes continue to relay and index transactions as usual, ignoring the OP_RETURN payload and treating the P2SH carrier outputs as ordinary 1-DOGE P2SH UTXOs.

Phase 2 is expected to be a consensus soft fork and must be specified with full backwards-compatibility and activation analysis in a successor BIP.

==Security Considerations==

Phase 1 commitments provide publication and detectability, not script-level ZK enforcement.
Users must not interpret commitment publication alone as equivalent to consensus-enforced proof validity.

Canonical encoding requirements are mandatory to avoid ambiguous parsing and commitment spoofing via non-standard script shapes. In particular:

* SPV scanners MUST reject ZK OP_RETURN scripts whose mode byte is not in the allow-list ({0x00, 0x01, 0x02} at this revision).
* SPV scanners MUST reject ZKP1 payloads whose magic is wrong, whose mode disagrees with the OP_RETURN mode, or whose length fields are inconsistent.
* The 32 MiB cap on <code>PROOF_LEN4</code> bounds resource consumption during reassembly and verification.

For replay protection, applications that need transaction-binding (rather than just proof-binding) MUST include the spending sighash, the spending tx Merkle root, or another suitable binding in the proof's public inputs; otherwise a Phase 1 commitment-match alone does not prevent reusing a valid proof in a different spend context.

For verifier-key trust, Phase 1 mandates ZKP1 v1 for new deployments: the verification key is embedded in the same canonical byte string that produces <code>commit32 = SHA256d(ZK_PAYLOAD)</code>, so the on-chain commitment covers the vk in addition to the proof and public inputs, and a verifier can recover the entire validation tuple <code>(public_inputs, proof, vk)</code> from a single TX_R reveal. ZKP1 v0 remains accepted by decoders for backwards compatibility with reveals published before v1; deployers MUST NOT publish new v0 reveals because they require an out-of-band <code>vk</code> channel that is outside the consensus-relevant binding and therefore vulnerable to verifier-substitution attacks.

For DELEGATED verification, the <code>Reveal validated</code> line indicates only that <code>SHA256d(ZK_PAYLOAD)</code> matches the on-chain commitment — it does not assert proof validity. Auditors and high-assurance verifiers SHOULD run an in-process or off-box verification step before acting on a "DELEGATED" outcome.

===External verifier hand-off (PLONK and other DELEGATED modes)===

Because libdogecoin does not ship a native PLONK verifier (and does not ship a native Groth16 verifier unless built with rapidsnark), Phase 1's PLONK verification path is intentionally an external-verifier hand-off:

# spvnode walks the chain, matches the TX_R reveal payload against a buffered TX_C commitment, and emits <code>[zk-commit] Reveal validated: TX_R=...</code> for the matched pair.
# An end-to-end driver (e.g. <code>contrib/zk_carrier/scripts/broadcast_set.sh</code>) MUST then invoke the canonical external verifier — for PLONK that is <code>snarkjs plonk verify recovered_vk.json public.json proof.json</code> against the vk recovered from the on-chain reveal — and tee the verifier's transcript into the curated mainnet log alongside the spvnode lines.
# The external verifier MUST be invoked AFTER spvnode emits <code>Reveal validated</code> for the corresponding pair, so that an auditor reading the log can correlate the on-chain commitment match with the off-box cryptographic check.

This split — commitment matching enforced by spvnode, proof validity attested by an external verifier — preserves libdogecoin's policy of keeping prover and verifier code paths out of consensus while still giving operators a single end-to-end log that demonstrates both halves of "<code>SHA256d(ZK_PAYLOAD)</code> matched the on-chain commit AND the proof verifies under the revealed vk".

==Reference Implementation==

Current reference behavior exists in libdogecoin components that:

* encode/decode the canonical ZKP1 payload via <code>dogecoin_zk_encode_payload</code> / <code>dogecoin_zk_decode_payload</code>;
* compute commitments via <code>dogecoin_zk_get_commitment_hash</code> (<code>SHA256d</code> over the ZKP1 payload);
* build canonical OP_RETURN scripts via <code>dogecoin_zk_build_opreturn_scriptpubkey</code>;
* attach the OP_RETURN commitment and P2SH carrier outputs in one operation via <code>dogecoin_zk_build_carrier_tx_c</code> (and the matching <code>such -c zk_add_commit_and_carrier_tx</code> CLI);
* extract the canonical OP_RETURN commitment from a TX_C via <code>dogecoin_tx_extract_zk_commit</code>;
* reassemble the ZK payload from TX_R scriptSigs via <code>dogecoin_zk_extract_carrier_payload</code>;
* dispatch to per-mode verifiers via <code>dogecoin_zk_verify_proof</code>, with rapidsnark- or mcl-backed in-process Groth16 verification gated on <code>HAVE_RAPIDSNARK</code> / <code>HAVE_MCL</code>;
* keep proving out-of-process per libdogecoin policy: <code>dogecoin_zk_generate_groth16_proof</code> / <code>dogecoin_zk_generate_plonk_proof</code> always return <code>DOGECOIN_ZK_ERR_DELEGATED</code>; the supported proving path is <code>contrib/zk_carrier/witness_helper.py</code> driving snarkjs or the rapidsnark CLI on the host;
* detect canonical ZK commitments during SPV block scanning, buffer them as pending TX_C commits, and emit deterministic <code>[zk-commit]</code> log lines on TX_R reveal.

====Prover / Verifier Support Matrix====

The following matrix lists which proof systems libdogecoin natively supports (in-process inside the C library), which are delegated to off-box tooling, and which configure-time options enable each path. Proving is always delegated, by policy, to keep libdogecoin mobile-friendly; verification is the only path that can run in-process.

{|
! Proof system !! Native prover (in-library) !! Native verifier (in-library) !! Delegated verifier (off-box) !! Configure flags !! depends opt-in
|-
| Groth16 (BN254 / snarkjs <code>"curve": "bn128"</code> / herumi/mcl <code>BN_SNARK1</code>; classical security only, '''not''' post-quantum safe) || none — DELEGATED || rapidsnark or herumi/mcl, dispatched by <code>dogecoin_zk_verify_proof</code> || snarkjs CLI || <code>--enable-zk-carrier</code> AND (<code>--with-rapidsnark</code> OR <code>--with-mcl[=DIR]</code>) || <code>ZK_CARRIER=1</code>
|-
| PLONK (BN254 / snarkjs <code>"curve": "bn128"</code>; classical security only, '''not''' post-quantum safe) || none — DELEGATED || none — <code>dogecoin_zk_verify_proof</code> returns DELEGATED || snarkjs CLI (canonical PLONK reference) || <code>--enable-zk-carrier</code> only || none required
|-
| STARK_S2 (hash/FRI; post-quantum-safe growth slot — see "PQC-safe growth path" under Rationale) || none — DELEGATED || none — <code>dogecoin_zk_verify_proof</code> returns NOT_IMPLEMENTED || none yet || <code>--enable-zk-carrier</code> reserves the mode byte || N/A (reserved)
|}

Native Groth16 verification is wired in two interchangeable backends — rapidsnark (<code>--with-rapidsnark</code>) and herumi/mcl (<code>--with-mcl[=DIR]</code>); both consume the same snarkjs JSON encoding embedded in the ZKP1 payload, so a build with either flag will verify the identical on-chain reveal end-to-end. Without either flag, Groth16 verification falls through to <code>DELEGATED</code> exactly like PLONK, and the off-box snarkjs invocation in <code>contrib/zk_carrier/scripts/</code> still validates against on-chain bytes alone.

Current CLI wiring reflects this implementation model:

* <code>such -c zk_commit</code> derives the canonical OP_RETURN script and 32-byte commit from a payload hex;
* <code>such -c zk_add_commit_and_carrier_tx</code> attaches both the OP_RETURN commitment and the P2SH carrier outputs in one call, and prints <code>carrier_part_total</code>, <code>carrier_first_vout</code>, and <code>carrier_part_scriptsig[i]</code> for the operator to assemble TX_R;
* <code>such -c zk_extract_carrier</code> reassembles and prints the ZK payload hex from a TX_R for round-trip cross-checking against what was published.

Current end-to-end script behavior is aligned with the parallel PQC mainnet flow (<code>contrib/mainnet_falcon_test.sh</code>):

* the <code>contrib/zk_carrier/scripts/run_full_zk_carrier_demo.sh</code> driver supports commitment-only and carrier modes; auto-discovers the funding UTXO from public block explorers (dogechain.info / blockcypher / blockchair fallbacks); inherits the public demo WIF/address from the PQC scripts; and watches the <code>spvnode -d</code> log for the <code>[zk-commit] Reveal validated</code> marker as the success criterion;
* <code>spvnode</code>'s <code>-p</code> (checkpoint) flag uses the most recent baked-in mainnet checkpoint to skip the genesis-to-checkpoint header sync, keeping the validation roundtrip to minutes once fees are properly sized.

==Mainnet Examples==

The following examples are from confirmed Dogecoin mainnet transactions demonstrating Phase 1 ZK carrier commitment publication, P2SH carrier reveal, and full on-chain proof validation. Both examples publish ZKP1 v1 reveals (verification key embedded in the canonical payload), so the validation tuple <code>(public_inputs, proof, vk)</code> is recovered from a single TX_R reveal and verified against on-chain bytes only — no out-of-band <code>verification_key.json</code> is consulted at any step. The format mirrors the Falcon-512 / Dilithium2 / Raccoon-G mainnet examples in the parallel PQC BIP.

A recent mainnet checkpoint is baked in at height 6191600 (hash <code>a8eeeadc81e8def69167f0d82c50c4d05fdb0c6ffd17a6af955f80d6925e0f0d</code>, 2026-05-03T18:54:38Z) so <code>spvnode -p</code> rescans of these and subsequent ZK carrier pairs only need to sync the final ~30 blocks of headers before reaching the published commitments.

===ZKP1 v1 self-contained reveal — end-to-end validation log (Groth16 + PLONK, with tx_base binding)===

ZKP1 v1 closes the v0 out-of-band-vk gap: when the encoder is invoked with <code>--vkey</code>, the verification key bytes are embedded inside the canonical payload so the on-chain reveal alone is sufficient to verify the proof end-to-end. Because the vk is part of the canonical byte string hashed to <code>commit32</code>, swapping the vk after publication invalidates the OP_RETURN commitment match — the consensus-relevant binding extends to the verifier material itself.

In addition to embedding the vk, the v1 e2e flow now binds every proof to the funding transaction's tx_base sighash (<code>tx_binding</code> public input, defined in §"Phase 1: Base Transaction Binding" above). This mirrors the PQC carrier's signature-over-tx_base model and is independently checked by snarkjs at verify time and by spvnode at scan time.

The v1 reveal flow is exercised by <code>contrib/zk_carrier/scripts/v1_reveal_e2e.sh</code>, which:

# constructs a synthetic TX_C base (single-vin, single-vout to the funded address <code>DDMpdcTrWnZT38tRMebbYzCSAgLSnVMqvr</code>; live UTXO state at <code>https://chain.so/address/DOGE/DDMpdcTrWnZT38tRMebbYzCSAgLSnVMqvr</code>) and computes <code>tx_base_sighash = sighash32(BASE_UNSIGNED, signer_p2pkh_spk, in_idx=0, hashtype=SIGHASH_ALL)</code> via <code>such -c tx_sighash32</code>, top-byte-zeroed to fit BN254;
# generates a real Groth16/PLONK range-proof against the in-tree <code>contrib/zk_carrier/circuits/range_proof.circom</code> (witness <code>amount=50000</code> in <code>[0, 1000000]</code>, <code>tx_binding</code> set to the value from step 1) via <code>witness_helper.py --vkey ... --proof-system &lt;groth16|plonk&gt; --tx-binding-hex ...</code>;
# encodes the ZKP1 v1 payload (version byte = <code>0x01</code>; trailing <code>VK_LEN4 || VK</code> block);
# attaches OP_RETURN + P2SH carrier outputs through <code>such -c zk_add_commit_and_carrier_tx</code>;
# constructs a synthetic TX_R, attaches each <code>zk_carrier_part_scriptsig</code> via <code>such -c set_scriptsig</code>, then reassembles the payload with <code>such -c zk_extract_carrier</code> (which now also emits <code>zk_vk_len</code> / <code>zk_vk_hex</code> for v1 reveals);
# materializes <code>(public.json, proof.json, vk.json)</code> from the extracted bytes only — no out-of-band file is read at this step — and runs <code>snarkjs &lt;system&gt; verify</code>;
# recomputes <code>tx_base_sighash</code> from TX_C and asserts equality with <code>public_inputs[2]</code> of the recovered payload — emitting the same <code>[zk-commit] tx_binding match</code> log line that <code>spvnode</code> emits in production;
# performs a replay-attack tamper test: builds an alternate funding tx with a different <code>vout[0].value</code>, swaps the <code>tx_binding</code> public input to that alternate sighash, and asserts that <code>snarkjs verify</code> rejects the lifted proof under the new binding.

