Both runs demonstrate three independent validation properties of v1 with tx_base binding:

# '''Self-contained verification.''' <code>SHA256d(ZK_PAYLOAD)</code> binds the OP_RETURN commitment to the exact <code>(public_inputs, proof, vk)</code> tuple that downstream tooling consumes (<code>snarkjs verify</code> here; equivalently <code>spvnode</code> with <code>HAVE_RAPIDSNARK</code> + the recovered vk, or any third-party verifier) — no out-of-band <code>verification_key.json</code> channel is involved at any step.
# '''Tx_base binding.''' The third public input <code>tx_binding == 008ea0b4356f2bb7df995106907c73ebf983e8184db054887564207bd5ebe607</code> matches the sighash <code>spvnode</code> recomputes from the matched TX_C bytes, exactly mirroring the PQC carrier's signature-over-tx_base scheme. The same value is reproduced by an external auditor running <code>such -c tx_sighash32</code> on the on-chain TX_C raw bytes.
# '''Replay protection.''' The Step-8 tamper test demonstrates that a lifted <code>(proof, vk)</code> tuple republished under a different funding tx (different <code>tx_base_sighash</code>, e.g. <code>0031fcf84beeb0699740e486c0562ec95bb0788ce63a5cbff77808427a454153</code>) is rejected by <code>snarkjs verify</code> — the same rejection an SPV node performs when it computes <code>[zk-commit] tx_binding mismatch</code> and refuses to log <code>Reveal validated</code>.

The end-to-end driver script (<code>contrib/zk_carrier/scripts/v1_reveal_e2e.sh</code>) and the synthetic offline-SPV unit test that exercises <code>dogecoin_zk_compute_tx_base_sighash</code> against a constructed TX_C+TX_R pair (<code>test_zk_tx_base_sighash_binding</code> in <code>test/zk_carrier_tests.c</code>) are committed in-tree so reviewers can reproduce all three validation paths bit-for-bit.

Live <code>spvnode</code> mainnet validation (which connects to peers and consumes the funded UTXOs at <code>DDMpdcTrWnZT38tRMebbYzCSAgLSnVMqvr</code>) is driven separately by <code>contrib/zk_carrier/scripts/broadcast_set.sh</code>; the corresponding <code>[zk-commit] Pending</code> → <code>Reveal validated</code> log progression for a real broadcast against the same address appears in the v1 cascade section below. Because the consensus-relevant <code>tx_binding</code> check runs on TX_C bytes that are identical between the offline driver and a real broadcast, the validation logs above are bit-identical to what <code>spvnode</code> emits during a live rescan once the corresponding TX_C+TX_R pair is mined.

