===Reproducing the on-chain-only validation===

The two outputs above (the <code>spvnode</code> rescan log and the independent <code>validate_onchain_pairs.py</code> summary) are produced by the following exact command sequence. Anyone with a Linux/macOS host, <code>node</code>, and a libdogecoin checkout can re-run it against the same Dogecoin mainnet artifacts cited above — the v1 pairs at block heights 6191613 / 6191616 (<code>TX_C=b70bc69f…e18354</code> + <code>TX_R=68e6d111…cf14d7</code> for Pair G1, <code>TX_C=d0a09969…2b4edc</code> + <code>TX_R=00333424…d82b5</code> for Pair Q1) and the v2 pairs at block 6205433 (<code>TX_C=c7058e41…b537d8</code> + <code>TX_R=5288fb5c…d892ad</code> for Pair G2, <code>TX_C=af37e7b8…077f60</code> + <code>TX_R=e963a3f3…f0a42</code> for Pair Q2) — and obtain a bit-identical trace; no local snapshot file is required.

The two steps cover complementary slices of the verification and must be read together:

* '''Step 1 (spvnode rescan)''' is the chain-walker side. From chain bytes alone it (a) reassembles the chunked <code>ZKP1FULL</code> payload from each TX_R's carrier scriptSigs, (b) checks <code>SHA256d(payload) == commit32</code> against the matching TX_C OP_RETURN, and (c) recomputes <code>tx_binding == dogecoin_zk_compute_tx_base_sighash(TX_C)</code> for v2 cascade pairs. No local verification-key parameter is used for this on-chain validation path.
* '''Step 2 (<code>validate_onchain_pairs.py</code>)''' is what actually backs the "on-chain only" claim cryptographically. It pulls the raw TX_C/TX_R hex from a public block explorer, extracts the embedded <code>(public_inputs, proof, vk)</code> tuple from the reassembled reveal, writes the '''recovered''' vk to a temp file, and invokes <code>snarkjs … verify recovered_vk.json …</code> against it — i.e. the vk it verifies under was decoded from the TX_R bytes, not from any file on disk, exactly as described in the "Per-pair on-chain decomposition" section above (<code>vk=EMBEDDED reveal</code>). No local <code>verification_key.json</code> is consulted in Step 2.

A future libdogecoin build may let Step 1 do the cryptographic proof check against the inlined vk in-process for the existing pairing-based modes — closing the residual classical-security gap by routing the BN254 / <code>"curve": "bn128"</code> verification through the in-process <code>--with-mcl</code> / <code>--with-rapidsnark</code> backend instead of the off-box snarkjs invocation in Step 2. This is a defense-in-depth refactor for the Phase-1 classical modes, '''not''' the BIP's PQC-safe growth path: that path runs through mode <code>0x02</code> <code>DOGECOIN_ZK_MODE_STARK_S2</code> (hash/FRI; see "PQC-safe growth path" under Rationale) and through the sibling Post-Quantum Signature Commitments BIP, whose Falcon-512 / Dilithium2 / Raccoon-G-44 verifiers already run in-library and provide post-quantum-safe spend authorization today.

<source lang="bash">
# 0. Build libdogecoin with the ZK carrier enabled. Add --with-mcl (and/or
#    --with-rapidsnark) here if you want spvnode to report
#    "ZK verification PASSED" in-process instead of "DELEGATED" for Groth16;
#    PLONK always reports DELEGATED because libdogecoin has no in-tree PLONK
#    verifier (rapidsnark v0.0.8 upstream is Groth16-only).
./autogen.sh
./configure --enable-zk-carrier   # optionally: --with-mcl --with-rapidsnark
make -j

# 1. spvnode mainnet rescan against the funded carrier address. The -p flag
#    consumes the baked-in checkpoint at height 6191600 so the rescan only
#    syncs the final ~30 headers before reaching Pairs G1 and Q1.
./spvnode -l -p -b -d \
    -a DDMpdcTrWnZT38tRMebbYzCSAgLSnVMqvr \
    scan 2>&1 | tee /tmp/spvnode_zk_rescan.log

#    Grep only the consensus-relevant carrier lines for the curated log:
grep -E '\[zk-commit\] (Pending|Valid|reveal_decoded|ZK verification|Reveal validated)' \
    /tmp/spvnode_zk_rescan.log

# 2. Independent on-chain audit — fetches each TX_C/TX_R as raw hex from
#    api.blockchair.com/dogecoin, reassembles the ZKP1 payload, recomputes
#    SHA256d(payload) against the OP_RETURN commit, extracts the embedded
#    (public_inputs, proof, vk) tuple from the reveal bytes themselves, writes
#    the recovered vk to a temp file, and invokes the canonical snarkjs
#    verifier against it. The on-chain-only cryptographic verdict comes from
#    THIS step: the vk fed to snarkjs is the one decoded out of TX_R, not the
#    local contrib/zk_carrier/circuits/build/verification_key.json file.
npm install snarkjs                              # one-time, vendored locally
SNARKJS="$PWD/node_modules/.bin/snarkjs" \
    python3 contrib/zk_carrier/scripts/validate_onchain_pairs.py \
    2>&1 | tee /tmp/validate_onchain_pairs.log

#    The underlying per-pair invocation the script issues — emitted verbatim
#    so an auditor can run it by hand on the recovered files — is:
#
#      snarkjs groth16 verify recovered_vk.json recovered_public.json recovered_proof.json   # Pair G1
#      snarkjs plonk   verify recovered_vk.json recovered_public.json recovered_proof.json   # Pair Q1
#
#    Both calls MUST exit 0 and print "[INFO]  snarkJS: OK!" — that line plus
#    "PASS  (full end-to-end verification (v1 self-contained reveal))" is the
#    on-chain-only validation success indicator for the respective proof
#    system.

# 3. (Optional) Combine the two transcripts into a single curated artifact
#    keyed by the on-chain identifiers cited above (TX_C/TX_R txids, block
#    heights, commit32) so a reviewer can re-fetch the same chain bytes and
#    reproduce every line independently.
</source>

The validator and spvnode read complementary slices of the on-chain bytes — spvnode walks the chain locally and emits the <code>[zk-commit] reveal_decoded</code> trace (commitment binding and <code>tx_binding</code> equality, both from chain bytes alone), while <code>validate_onchain_pairs.py</code> pulls the same TX_C/TX_R raw hex from a public block explorer, recovers the <code>vk</code> from the embedded reveal, and runs the cryptographic <code>snarkjs verify</code> against that recovered vk. A passing run with the commands above demonstrates the property end-to-end from two independent code paths.


==Deployment==

Phase 1 can be deployed immediately in wallets, tooling, and SPV infrastructure without consensus change.

Phase 2 requires a separate consensus-track proposal and activation plan, ideally co-sequenced with the PQC carrier's Phase 2 to share an OP_SUCCESSx allocation block.

